.DS_Store
._*
.AppleDouble
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
.fuse_hidden*
.directory

# Owner-key material must never enter the repository; custody is the OS keychain.
malik_seed.hex
malik.pem
malik.pub

# `mirsa-isdar.txt` is deliberately NOT listed here, and that is a decision
# someone should confirm rather than inherit.
#
# `isdar wallid --mirsa mirsa-isdar.txt` (docs/mustawda.md §7.1) writes exactly
# one file: 64 hexadecimal characters, the *public* half of the release signing
# key. The private half is generated into the platform credential store and
# never touches the filesystem, and the tool refuses an anchor path whose name
# reads as a private-key file — which is why the name carries no `.key` or
# `.pem` suffix, and therefore why none of the key rules above catch it.
#
# For committing it: it is the anchor every release build is compiled against,
# and a repository that does not record which anchor a tag was built with
# cannot answer "was this release signed by the key we think it was" after the
# fact. Against: a key-shaped file in a repository invites someone to assume
# the next one is equally safe.
#
# If the decision goes the other way, add `mirsa-isdar.txt` below this comment.
# Do not add it silently — a public anchor that quietly stops being published
# is worse than one that never was, because the release notes still cite it.

# The staged resource tree is build output. The directory itself is committed
# (tauri-build requires the declared resource path to exist on every build);
# what taarib-tajmee stages into it is not.
apps/studio/src-tauri/mawarid/*
!apps/studio/src-tauri/mawarid/README.md
